anding. To bridge this gap, we propose FATE,\n                                          Frame-level Audio-visual Temporal Embedding. Unlike prior\n                                          embedding models that pool each modality into a single                                    Semantic Discrimination\n                                          embedding and discard temporal information, FATE retains\n                                                                                                                      Synchformer      Video1: 0.25 <           Video2: 0.34\n                                          frame-level sequences, aligns them on the physical timeline,\n                                          and computes similarity over strictly aligned frame pairs. Un-                FATE          Video1: 0.14 >      Video2: 0.04\n                                          like synchronization models that output only an offset predic-                   Sync Model Fails       FATE succeeds\n                                          tion, FATE encodes synchronization in a reusable embedding\n                                                                                                                      Open Mouth Video 1              Close Mouth Video 2\n                                          space, trained with a joint objective combining cross-video\n                                          semantic and within-video temporal contrastive learning to\n                                          capture both what sounds and when it occurs. Across three\n                                          tasks, FATE surpasses the strongest baseline on temporal and\n                                          semantic retrieval by a large margin, matches fully super-\n                                          vised methods on event localization in a zero-shot setting,\n                                          and achieves the best correlation with human judgments as a